I've written the plan as two task files plus the issue responses, all in session_plan/. The commit step did nothing: session_plan/ is in .gitignore, so the files are on disk but not committed. I left it that way rather than force-add them, on the assumption that the harness reads the files from disk.

Task 1: upgrade yoagent from 0.25.16 to 0.26.x (Part of #1122, evolve). @yuanhao asked for this directly. The first step is reading the yoagent skill's "What changed" notes for 0.26.0–0.26.6, which was the main point of #1122. After that:
• Move the pin to "0.26".
• Register the new 0.26.4 presets in PRESETS_NOT_YET_ROUTED. Two existing tests are expected to fail on them.
• Re-read all 32 yoagent-version-claim: 0.25 paragraphs against 0.26. Each is either re-marked 0.26 or rewritten if 0.26 made it false.
• Mark the two hand-written fixes from Day 247 with the upstream issues that replace them.

This task goes past the 3-file limit, and the task file says why. tests/doc_version_claims.rs fails until every marker is re-read, so the version bump and the re-marking can't land as separate green tasks. Outside Cargo.toml, Cargo.lock and src/vendor_preset_tests.rs, the extra edits are comments and prose.

Task 2: stop retrying a DNS failure on a reserved name (Fixes #1120, product). This follows @yuanhao's decision (a) on #1120. Hosts ending in .invalid, .test, .example or .localhost can never resolve, so yoyo should stop retrying them right away. The measured case currently burns about 73 seconds before giving up. Ordinary DNS failures keep their retries, because a real resolver outage can be temporary. The task:
• Builds it in a new src/prompt/reserved_dns.rs, copying the loopback fast-fail from Day 247.
• Tests it against the real error text captured from the built binary, not a guessed string.
• Covers the edge cases in the table: example.com (a real domain, not the .example name), invalidator.dev, a reserved host with a non-DNS error, and an ordinary NXDOMAIN.
• Puts the reason as a comment next to the retry classifier, as he asked.

This slot also counts as the backlog drain: 16 self-filed issues are open, and this one closes outright.

